philosophy:
Capability, not ceremony. The bare model with host tools is a strong baseline. It scored about 99/100 on familiar topics in the v0.0.3 exam. On Claude Code, the bundled /deep-research workflow already fans out searches and votes on claims. VAFER DeepResearch should therefore contain only procedures that the model does not reliably perform on its own, and that published ablations or VAFER's own runs link to better outcomes:
- more searching and reading in parallel, in isolated contexts, on hard questions;
- a better first move (split the question into clues and assign lenses);
- a small plan that is rewritten each round and steers each wave;
- an independent, fresh-context check for gaps;
- tracing answer-like summaries back to primary records;
- one targeted, fresh-context verification pass that runs early enough to change the report;
- a single writer who builds the report section by section from cited notes;
- reuse of earlier research in the project.

Rules that follow from this:
- Effort is proportional to difficulty. An easy question gets the bare model plus a few rules, with no files, no subagents and no setup.
- Nothing runs after the report unless it can change the report.
- Integrity is a property of the output (claims checked against the sources they cite), not of records about the output.
- Every component can be switched off, has a stated cost, and stays only if a paired evaluation on tasks that defeat the model's memory shows that it helps.
- Harness assumptions go stale as models improve (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/harness-design-long-running-apps). Each component is re-tested on new model releases and removed when the model no longer needs it.
- When in doubt, delete.

This directly reverses the 0.0.1–0.0.3 pattern, in which every observed failure was answered with more record-keeping, and the optimisation target was unit-test counts and integrity invariants rather than measured research outcomes against a control.

helper_scope:
The skill works fully without the helper, and the helper never blocks delivery. It is one optional script, `scripts/vafer_dr.py`: Python 3.10+ standard library only, ≤300 lines. It uses pypdf if installed, or pdftotext if it is on PATH. It has two subcommands.

**1. `fetch URL|PATH ... [--out DIR] [--timeout 20] [--max-mb 5]`**
- Fetching:
  - fetches up to 6 URLs in parallel, following ≤5 redirects;
  - refuses non-http(s) schemes and private, loopback or link-local addresses (SSRF and exfiltration guard);
  - never executes page content.
- Conversion:
  - HTML to text with the existing extract.py HTMLParser block logic and charset decoding (reused code);
  - PDF to text when an extractor exists;
  - local PDF or HTML files are converted the same way, for finite-work reading.
- Status classes:
  - ok;
  - blocked (401/403/429/451, or bot walls such as Anubis or 'unusual traffic/anomaly' pages);
  - consent wall;
  - empty (<500 chars of body text);
  - error.
- Output files: one file per input, `DIR/<host>-<slug>-<sha8>.txt`, with a 5-line header (url, final_url, fetched_at UTC, status, sha256).
- Console output: one line per input, e.g. `ok 48213ch sources/rfc-editor-org-rfc9002-1a2b3c4d.txt`.
- No index file, database, locks or JSON dumps. Parallel researchers therefore never share mutable state, and nothing large flows back into the context.

**2. `check REPORT.md [--sources DIR] [--max 30]`**
- Rebuilds the URL→file map from the file headers. URLs are normalised: scheme, www, trailing slash, fragments and tracking parameters.
- Flags:
  - quoted spans of ≥4 words missing from the cited text;
  - numbers and dates in cited sentences missing from the cited text;
  - paragraphs with numbers or dates but no citation;
  - citations to unarchived pages.
- Prints ≤30 lines plus a one-line total, and always exits 0. Its output goes to the lead and the verifier. It is a lint that finds misquotes and mislinks; it does not decide truth.

**Tests:** about 10 offline unit tests with a local http.server and the existing fixtures, covering blocked, consent and empty pages, the PDF path, injection text treated as data, and a quote or number miss.

**Explicit non-goals (deleted on purpose):**
- web search;
- passage refs, revisions, notes or run state;
- coverage records, audits, reviews, verify/finish;
- status and next actions, batch, FTS index;
- capture-window warnings;
- a doctor command.

**Possible later addition, only if the evaluation shows the no-search-tool fallback matters:** `lookup --source openalex|crossref|arxiv|wikipedia|hn "query"`, printing ≤10 title | year | URL lines. On 2026-10-27 all five endpoints returned HTTP 200 to curl from this machine, while DuckDuckGo HTML returned a bot challenge, so general web search must not be scraped.

**Host notes:**
- Claude Code: running it is a Bash command. One approval of the command pattern can be saved by the user to the project's `.claude/settings.local.json`.
- Codex: shell network is off by default in workspace-write, so fetch needs approval. In non-interactive `codex exec` it fails cleanly, and the researcher records URL plus quote as 'unarchived'.
